Sure macha 😎 Here’s the Vector difference in C++, Java, and Python.

Feature	C++	Java	Python

Name	vector	ArrayList	list
Import	<vector>	java.util.ArrayList	No import needed
Type	Dynamic array	Dynamic array	Dynamic, flexible list
Syntax	vector<int> v;	ArrayList<Integer> v = new ArrayList<>();	v = []
Add	v.push_back(10)	v.add(10)	v.append(10)
Access	v[0]	v.get(0)	v[0]
Size	v.size()	v.size()	len(v)
Remove last	v.pop_back()	v.remove(v.size()-1)	v.pop()
Can store primitives?	Yes	No, uses wrapper (Integer)	Yes, dynamically typed
Type safety	Strong	Strong	Dynamic typing


1. C++ — vector

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    vector<int> v;

    v.push_back(10);
    v.push_back(20);
    v.push_back(30);

    cout << v[0] << endl;
    cout << v.size() << endl;
}

Output:

10
3

2. Java — ArrayList

import java.util.*;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> v = new ArrayList<>();

        v.add(10);
        v.add(20);
        v.add(30);

        System.out.println(v.get(0));
        System.out.println(v.size());
    }
}

Output:

10
3

3. Python — list

v = []

v.append(10)
v.append(20)
v.append(30)

print(v[0])
print(len(v))

Output:

10
3

🔥 Easy way to remember

C++

> vector → push_back() → size()



Java

> ArrayList → add() → size()



Python

> list → append() → len()



One important point: Java doesn't have a built-in Vector equivalent to C++'s vector in the usual modern usage. Java actually has a class literally called Vector, but for most DSA work, ArrayList is the closer comparison to C++ vector and Python list.
